  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#dom-hyperlink-protocol:\r\n\r\nThe getter always returns something with a trailing colon:\r\n> 2. If this element's url is null, return \":\".\r\n> 3. Return this element's url's scheme, followed by \":\".\r\n\r\nWhile the setter sounds like (somehow) trying to pad a colon:\r\n> 3. Basic URL parse **the given value, followed by :\"**, with this element's url as *url* and scheme start state as *state override*.\r\n\r\nI may be misreading the setter's behavior, but still \"followed by\" instead of \"which ends with\" does sound weird in this case as it's talking adding a colon. In fact, Firefox's [`window.location` handling](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=726779) seems to use the strange interpretation: it tells you that `location.protocol` is `\"https:\"`, but the setter only happily accepts `\"https\"`.\r\n\r\nIs there any clarification for this trunk of text? That would help with writing proper bug reports if there is indeed a problem.\r\n\r\n* * *\r\n\r\n@lixiaoyan reported the Firefox problem informally in a group chat, where @Septs provided a pointer to WHATWG's specification that confused me. Thanks to both of them.",
